---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'signals', label: 'Signals' },
   { id: 'states', label: 'Applicability states' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Reading applicability' },
   { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits of applicability' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Applicability"
   summary="Applicability answers one question: does this page contain anything a given WCAG success criterion, one of its testable requirements, is about? a11ied reads the HTML for 15 kinds of signal, matches them against criterion tags, and returns a state for every criterion in the version."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="signals">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Signals</p>
            <h2>Applicability signals</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>a1 audit</code> fetches the target's HTML alongside its axe scan and accessibility
            tree, then parses that HTML for applicability signals. Each signal has a category,
            the source it came from, the text that matched, a confidence of high, medium, or low,
            and up to ten <code>elements</code>: the XPath, tag, and opening tag of the nodes that
            produced it.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"signals": [
  &#123;
    "category": "heading", "source": "dom", "value": "heading structure", "confidence": "high",
    "elements": [&#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/h1", "tag": "h1", "snippet": "&lt;h1&gt;" &#125;]
  &#125;,
  &#123;
    "category": "form", "source": "dom", "value": "form controls", "confidence": "high",
    "elements": [
      &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/form", "tag": "form", "snippet": "&lt;form action=\"/save\" method=\"post\"&gt;" &#125;,
      &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/form/label/input", "tag": "input", "snippet": "&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"displayName\"&gt;" &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
         <p>
            The 15 categories are <code>auth</code>, <code>dialog</code>,
            <code>drag-and-drop</code>, <code>form</code>, <code>heading</code>, <code>help</code>,
            <code>landmark</code>, <code>live-region</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>menu</code>,
            <code>overlay</code>, <code>repeated-form</code>, <code>tablist</code>,
            <code>validation</code>, and <code>widget</code>.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>landmark</code> and <code>live-region</code> come from ARIA. A landmark is a
            labeled region such as <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> that lets
            assistive tech jump straight to it. A live region is a page area assistive tech
            watches, so it can announce a change without the user navigating there.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Sources</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            A signal comes from the DOM, the accessibility tree, document metadata, a W3C Quickref
            tag, or a hint you supplied.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="states">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">States</p>
         <h2>Applicability states</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>applicable</code>
            <span>A signal matches the criterion's tags, strongly enough to call it applicable.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>not-detected</code>
            <span>Nothing on the page matched. The criterion may still apply.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>out-of-scope</code>
            <span>The criterion does not apply to this kind of target.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>unknown</code>
            <span>The signals do not support a decision either way.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="commands">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Commands</p>
         <h2>Reading applicability</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>a1 audit</code> returns an <code>applicability</code> section with a matrix that
         has one assessment per criterion, alongside its axe scan and accessibility tree. The MCP
         server exposes the same matrix through its <code>applicability</code> tool, which takes an
         optional <code>criterion</code> to narrow the result to one assessment.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/dialog.html --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"applicability": &#123;
  "matrix": &#123;
    "assessments": &#123;
      "2.4.3": &#123;
        "criterionId": "2.4.3",
        "title": "Focus Order",
        "state": "applicable",
        "reasons": [
          "Detected landmark, heading, form, dialog structure, and fixed or overlay signals (landmark structure, heading structure, form controls, and dialog structure) and matching criterion tags (navigation, structure, forms, controls, errors, modals, focus, keyboard, and menus)."
        ],
        "matchedSignalCategories": ["landmark", "heading", "form", "dialog", "overlay"],
        "matchedTags": ["navigation", "structure", "forms", "controls", "errors", "modals", "focus", "keyboard", "menus"],
        "elements": [
          &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main", "tag": "main", "snippet": "&lt;main&gt;" &#125;,
          &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/h1", "tag": "h1", "snippet": "&lt;h1&gt;" &#125;,
          &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/div/div[2]", "tag": "div", "snippet": "&lt;div role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-label=\"Preferences\"&gt;" &#125;
        ]
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         The page opens a hidden <code>Preferences</code> dialog on a button click. Its markup
         still parses, so the dialog signal counts even before the click runs.
         <code>reasons</code> lists the signals and the tags that produced the state. Read it
         before acting on the state.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>elements</code> lists the nodes behind the assessment, merged across the matched
         signals and capped at 20. Paste an XPath into the browser console as
         <code>$x('/html/body/main')</code> to jump to the node. The text output shows the
         first few with <code>--verbose</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="limits">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Limits</p>
         <h2>The limits of applicability</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         An assessment never says pass or fail. It says which criteria are worth testing on this
         page, so a sweep of 86 criteria becomes a short list.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>not-detected</code> is the state to be careful with. The analyzer reads the HTML the
         server returned. Content a client framework renders later, or a dialog that only exists
         after a click, produces no signal. Use <a href="/coverage">coverage</a> to decide what
         evidence a criterion needs, and a driver session to reach states the initial HTML does not
         contain.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
